= Scientific method — Simple =

'''Scientific method''' means the organized ways scientists investigate questions about the world.

There is no single list of steps that every scientist always follows.

Scientific work can include:

* asking a question;
* making observations;
* taking [[Measurement/Simple|measurements]];
* proposing a [[Hypothesis/Simple|hypothesis]];
* building a [[Scientific model/Simple|model]];
* doing an experiment;
* comparing predictions with [[Evidence/Simple|evidence]];
* repeating or [[Replication/Simple|replicating]] work;
* changing the explanation when the evidence does not fit.

Different sciences use different methods.

A chemist can directly change ingredients in a laboratory. An astronomer cannot move a star, so astronomy often depends on careful observations and models.

Science is also not finished after one experiment. Results become stronger when methods are clear, other researchers can check the work, and new evidence continues to agree.

